select null, t.id, 'builtin', 'Which iron profile is typical of iron deficiency anemia?', '["High ferritin, low TIBC","Low serum iron, high TIBC, low ferritin","Normal serum iron, TIBC, and ferritin","Low serum iron, low TIBC, high ferritin"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Iron deficiency gives low serum iron, high TIBC, and low ferritin, with microcytic, hypochromic cells. Anemia of chronic disease has a high ferritin, and thalassemia has normal iron studies. Ferritin is an acute-phase reactant, so inflammation can hide a low value. Reference: StatPearls, Chronic Anemia (ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK568790) and Iron Deficiency and Microcytic Hypochromic Anemia (ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560876).', '["That pattern fits anemia of chronic disease or iron overload.","Stores run out first (low ferritin), so the body makes more transferrin (high TIBC).","A normal profile rules out iron deficiency.","Typical of anemia of chronic inflammation."]'::jsonb, 1, 'verified'
